---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import ExplanationList from "../../../components/story/ExplanationList.astro";
import DecisionTimeline from "../../../components/story/DecisionTimeline.astro";
import QuoteCard from "../../../components/story/QuoteCard.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import { claim } from "../../../lib/claims.mjs";
import { event, n, chronology, headlinesVsRule, steps, explanations } from "../../../data/ai-chip-exports-2026-11-16-commerce-requires-licenses-for-chips-at-china-serving-data-centers.mjs";
const story = "ai-chip-exports--2026-11-16-commerce-requires-licenses-for-chips-at-china-serving-data-centers";
const short = (id) => claim(story, id).short.replace(/^[A-Z][a-z]{2,3}\.? [0-9, ]*[0-9]{0,4}: /, "");
const cap = (s) => s.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + s.slice(1);
const timeline = chronology.map((e) => ({ date: e.date, title: cap(short(e.c)), current: e.current, cites: [{ c: e.c }] }));
---
<BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/ai-chip-exports/">AI chip exports</a><span>November 16, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">AI chip exports &middot; November 16, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updated}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}<Cite c="effective-on-publication" /><Cite c="abroad-only" /></p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#how-it-got-here">A rule promised for 18 months</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-wrote-it">Who wrote it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The Catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#explanations">Every explanation, tested</a></li>
          <li><a href="#coverage">What the coverage got right and wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">The Commerce Department on November 16 issued a rule that requires a U.S. license to ship advanced AI chips to a data center anywhere outside the United States that gives Chinese companies more than {n.threshold} "aggregate TPP," the combined processing performance of the chips it rents to them.<Cite c="rule-issued" /><Cite c="data-center-license" /><Cite c="tpp-defined" /> That is about {n.h100} of Nvidia's H100 chips, by the count of Chris McGuire, a former White House national security official; the rule itself gives no chip count.<Cite c="threshold-in-chips" /> The same rule requires a license to ship such chips at all to Indonesia, Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand and Brazil, unless the end user is a U.S. company and the exporter reports the sale.<Cite c="five-countries" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">The rule targets the way Chinese companies get around U.S. limits. They have needed a U.S. license to buy the most advanced chips since 2022, so many rent them instead, from data centers in Southeast Asia.<Cite c="direct-ban-2022" /><Cite c="rent-abroad" /> In May, the Commerce Department's export agency, the Bureau of Industry and Security (BIS), told data center operators that were following the rules that they did not have to stop using the chips they had, until further notice.<Cite c="may-31-tolerance" /> Now a data center already serving Chinese companies at that scale has {n.graceDays} days to get a license or face restrictions, and the rule reaches chips already installed, not only new shipments.<Cite c="existing-90-days" /><Cite c="installed-chips" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">The rule takes effect when it is published in the Federal Register, scheduled for November 19, and BIS will take comments for {n.commentDays} days after that.<Cite c="effective-on-publication" /><Cite c="comments-60-days" /> Commerce's own announcement says Treasury and the White House National Security Council wrote it.<Cite c="written-by-treasury-nsc" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="how-it-got-here">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">A rule promised for 18 months</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">BIS says the rule is meant to stop companies from getting around existing limits on American technology by renting computing power at data centers in other countries.<Cite c="bis-purpose" /> President Biden's administration wrote a rule to do that in January 2025, due to take effect that May; Trump's Commerce Department said it would not enforce it, and it never was.<Cite c="biden-rule-rescinded" /> BIS promised then to issue a replacement.<Cite c="replacement-promised" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">According to people who described the talks to The New York Times, top officials pressed in October for rules restricting Chinese access to computing power in Southeast Asia, the president signed off, and Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick said he did not understand the rush and warned the step would set off a further trade war with China.<Cite c="october-signoff" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Then came a scandal over one data center company. Megaspeed, based in Singapore, had been building data centers in Southeast Asia to serve Chinese clients, and U.S. authorities are investigating it over alleged chip smuggling.<Cite c="megaspeed-serves-china" /> On November 12, Nvidia's chief executive, Jensen Huang, stepped down after reports of his affair with Alice Huang, a former Megaspeed executive whom U.S. authorities consider a Chinese intelligence asset; China denies she is a spy.<Cite c="megaspeed-scandal" /> The next day Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent blamed Commerce for not putting Megaspeed on the entity list, a trade blacklist, and two senators wrote to Lutnick demanding limits on remote access.<Cite c="bessent-has-the-pen" /><Cite c="blunt-rochester-curtis" /> The rule came three days later.<Cite c="rule-issued" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>From the 2022 limits to the rule</strong></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={timeline} heading="What happened" />
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="who-wrote-it">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who wrote it</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The rule is a Commerce rule, signed by Jeffrey Kessler, the department's under secretary for export controls.<Cite c="kessler-signed" /> But Commerce's announcement says it was "written by and at the direction of the Department of Treasury and the National Security Council."<Cite c="written-by-treasury-nsc" /> Three people told Politico that Bessent pushed it through, with the support of Marco Rubio, the secretary of state and national security adviser.<Cite c="bessent-pushed" /> Lutnick had wanted a leaner rule, one less onerous for Nvidia and its customers, the Times reported.<Cite c="lutnick-leaner" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">McGuire, who praised the rule, singled out that line.<Cite c="mcguire-step-forward" /><Cite c="mcguire-unprecedented" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Chris McGuire, Council on Foreign Relations" when="November 16, 2026" where="Post on X">
          <p>"I have never seen a BIS rule that was not written by BIS."<Cite c="mcguire-unprecedented" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Commerce tells it differently. A spokeswoman said the department was already considering remote-access rules with other agencies, and on November 17 Commerce and the Justice Department said Commerce had been investigating Megaspeed since 2024.<Cite c="commerce-considering" /><Cite c="commerce-megaspeed-statement" /> Asked about the split, Trump said there were "two different views," and that Lutnick might have delayed action because otherwise "you could have had a blowup with China."<Cite c="trump-two-views" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The Catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>A license, not a ban, and only abroad</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">NBC's headline said the United States "closes loophole that let Chinese companies use top AI chips," and its story said remote access "will be banned."<Cite c="nbc-headline" /><Cite c="nbc-banned" /> The rule does neither. Its data-center control covers chips sent to data centers outside the United States, so renting advanced chips at a data center inside the country stays outside it.<Cite c="abroad-only" /> It regulates the chips, not the renting of computing power, as the compliance publisher Kharon puts it.<Cite c="regulates-chips-not-compute" /> Abroad, the new control applies only above {n.threshold} TPP; below that it requires no license unless BIS tells a company one is needed, though BIS says an older control still covers a data center whose main user is a Chinese company.<Cite c="below-threshold" /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">Where the rule does apply, it asks for a license, not a halt. Data-center licenses are reviewed case by case, while most chips meant for a Chinese-headquartered company itself face a presumption of denial: BIS starts from refusing them.<Cite c="case-by-case" /><Cite c="presumption-of-denial" /> BIS expects about {n.licenses} more licenses a year.<Cite c="about-100-licenses" /></p>
              <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
                <figcaption><strong>What the coverage said, and what the rule says</strong></figcaption>
                <table class="data-table catch-compare">
                  <thead><tr><th>Coverage said</th><th>The rule says</th></tr></thead>
                  <tbody>{headlinesVsRule.map((r) => <tr><td>{r.said}<Cite c={r.saidC} /></td><td>{r.rule}{r.ruleC.map((id) => <Cite c={id} />)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
                </table>
              </figure>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Who wanted it to go further</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">McGuire calls the U.S. exemption the rule's biggest gap: Chinese companies, he says, can still legally train on advanced chips "at data centers in Northern Virginia."<Cite c="us-data-centers-gap" /> Two senators, Lisa Blunt Rochester, a Delaware Democrat, and John Curtis, a Utah Republican, had asked Lutnick three days earlier for limits that are not geographically bound, warning that China would otherwise rent chips elsewhere, including through U.S. subsidiaries.<Cite c="blunt-rochester-curtis" /> The House passed a bill in January, the Remote Access Security Act, that would extend export controls to remote access to U.S. items by foreign persons; it awaits the Senate.<Cite c="rasa" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="explanations">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Every explanation, tested</h2>
        <p class="section-intro" data-layer="fact">What the rule does and why it came now, as each side explains it, with the evidence for and against.</p>
        <ExplanationList entries={explanations} />
      </section>

      <section id="coverage">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right and wrong</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>The Associated Press</strong> wrote that BIS issued "the rule that took effect immediately."<Cite c="ap-immediately" /> It takes effect when the Federal Register publishes it, scheduled for November 19.<Cite c="effective-on-publication" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>The New York Times</strong> wrote that the provision "goes into effect in 90 days."<Cite c="nyt-90-days" /> The 90 days is the deadline for data centers already serving Chinese firms at that scale to get a license; the rule itself takes effect on publication.<Cite c="existing-90-days" /><Cite c="effective-on-publication" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>The Wall Street Journal</strong>, in an earlier capture of its story, called it a "new proposed rule"; a later capture that day did not.<Cite c="wsj-proposed" /> It is an interim final rule, which takes effect on publication without a comment period first; comments come afterward.<Cite c="not-a-proposal" /><Cite c="effective-on-publication" /><Cite c="comments-60-days" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The dates ahead</strong></figcaption>
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{steps.map((s) => <tr><td>{s.when}</td><td>{s.what}{s.c.map((id) => <Cite c={id} />)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
          </table>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">China's commerce ministry says the rule departs from the understanding Trump and Xi reached and that China will take countermeasures if the United States persists.<Cite c="china-countermeasures" /> Singapore says it is talking to U.S. officials about access for its companies.<Cite c="singapore-trusted-access" /> Nvidia's board set up a committee on national security and export controls.<Cite c="nvidia-committee" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .story-figure .data-table td, .story-figure .data-table th { word-break: normal; overflow-wrap: normal; hyphens: none; }
  .catch-compare th:first-child, .catch-compare td:first-child { width: 34%; }
</style>
